| Author                            | Appleton et al, 2013 [29] Soriguer et al, 2013 [37] Achilike et al, 2015 [28] Bell et al, 2015 [30] Lee et al, 2015 [33]                                                                                                                                       | Hamer et al, 2015 [34] Hwang et al, 2015 Kim et al, 2016 [32] Kabat et al, 2017 [31] Eckel et al, 2018 [13]                                                                                                             | Mongraw-Chaffin et al, 2018 [35] Moussa et al, 2018 [36]                                                                                               |, ord=44], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0045, section=Determinants for conversion from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes, kind=caption, page=6, bbox={l=55.18063223872, t=411.0203061264634, r=62.63298964879286, b=713.87700159}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Table 2 Summary of prospective cohort studies on long-term stability of metabolic health, ord=45], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0046, section=Determinants for conversion from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes, kind=paragraph, page=6, bbox={l=418.39839124592, t=590.512597865, r=440.43448805879285, b=713.8770996596298}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=a Obesity defined as BMI ≥ 25 kg/m 2

b Obesity defined as BMI ≥ 35 kg/m 2, ord=46], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0047, section=Determinants for conversion from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes, kind=caption, page=6, bbox={l=445.89634059535996, t=378.4617765851996, r=453.24707960879283, b=713.87700159}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=MHNW, metabolically healthy normal-weight; MUHO, metabolically unhealthy obesity; ND, no data, ord=47], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0048, section=Outlook, kind=paragraph, page=7, bbox={l=51.02360152999999, t=632.5605538955, r=289.20644757307497, b=715.9279903535174}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Although an increasing number of prospective cohort studies have evaluated subgroups of obese and normal-weight individuals, the concept of metabolic health remains controversial. Epidemiological research could inform this debate, as shown in the Text box. In summary, it is hoped that ongoing research in this area will eventually allow clinicians and researchers to come to an agreement with regards, ord=48], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0049, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=title, page=7, bbox={l=341.80511474, t=65.09425513359997, r=512.9706242338001, b=105.74762907797492}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, ord=49], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0050, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=list, page=7, bbox={l=330.07681274, t=123.58422704001998, r=527.7406989549387, b=182.25387241423005}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Systematic evaluation of existing and alternative strategies to define metabolic health would help us to come to an agreement upon a common definition for metabolic health phenotypes, providing information on the metabolic risk factors that should be assessed and their thresholds for diagnosis., ord=50], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0051, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=list, page=7, bbox={l=330.07681274, t=191.57292182455, r=527.8135314517004, b=240.06356800216008}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Studies using repeated measures account for the time-varying nature of metabolic health and body weight. More research on the transition from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes over long periods of time would be informative., ord=51], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0052, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=list, page=7, bbox={l=330.0768127399999, t=249.2597679444401, r=527.7968589524415, b=307.9557384929801}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Identification of determinants, specifically modifiable lifestyle factors, for the transition from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes would help to identify targets for prevention. This might be specifically relevant for different groups according to body fatness., ord=52], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0053, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=list, page=7, bbox={l=330.0768127399999, t=317.1519384352601, r=528.0092139430055, b=385.8865079233902}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=The evaluation of body-fat distribution patterns as potential main determinants of metabolic health and subsequent cardiometabolic risk may substantiate the notion that BMI is suboptimal for the classification of individuals with regard to cardiometabolic risk and may point towards alternative anthropometric measures for clinical use., ord=53], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0054, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=list, page=7, bbox={l=330.0768127399999, t=395.2055573337102, r=528.0715164402351, b=433.5172043147203}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Comparative evaluation of continuous riskassessment strategies may highlight alternative approaches to the categorical classification of metabolically healthy/unhealthy individuals., ord=54], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0055, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=paragraph, page=7, bbox={l=306.1458539354801, t=492.07793631513994, r=544.2589617803036, b=550.5587983984054}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=to the definition of metabolic health so that it may be monitored in obese and normal-weight individuals. This may enable optimal targeting for the prevention of complications associated with poor metabolic health, including CVD, to reduce the associated health and cost burdens., ord=55], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0056, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=paragraph, page=7, bbox={l=306.14169311, t=565.70022721872, r=544.2928738355605, b=613.0039321723848}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Acknowledgements I thank Nathalie Eckel and Olga Kuxhaus (Department of Molecular Epidemiology, German Institute of Human Nutrition, Germany) for their support in data analysis related to the estimation of MHO prevalence within National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey III., ord=56], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0057, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=paragraph, page=7, bbox={l=306.14169311, t=627.4926809142879, r=544.260694676625, b=684.7295860025167}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Funding Work by the author is supported by a grant from the German Ministry of Education and Research (BMBF) and the State of Brandenburg (DZD grant 82DZD00302).

Duality of interest The author declares that there is no duality of interest associated with this manuscript., ord=57], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0058, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=paragraph, page=7, bbox={l=306.14169311, t=697.2367762639858, r=544.2674680508527, b=704.6891336740587}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Contribution statement The author was the sole contributor to this paper., ord=58], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0059, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=paragraph, page=8, bbox={l=51.02165115601204, t=57.720073765359984, r=289.22364226543993, b=114.8976993384988}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Open Access This article is distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons At t r ibut ion 4.0 Internat ional License (ht tp: / / creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons license, and indicate if changes were made., ord=59], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0060, section=Epidemiological research in the classification of metabolic health status, kind=paragraph, page=8, bbox={l=51.02165115601204, t=129.19167974088793, r=289.14742677432, b=146.7550673625948}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Publisher's Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations., ord=60], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0061, section=References, kind=list, page=8, bbox={l=55.33229827, t=194.23667532536, r=289.20703813695985, b=241.54884754724287}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Emerging Risk Factors Collaboration, Wormser D, Kaptoge S et al (2011) Separate and combined associations of body-mass index and abdominal adiposity with cardiovascular disease: collaborative analysis of 58 prospective studies. Lancet 377(9771):1085-1095. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(11)60105-0, ord=61], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0062, section=References, kind=list, page=8, bbox={l=55.33229556017611, t=245.19830013945398, r=289.1816311667041, b=292.5104723613368}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Global BMI Mortality Collaboration, Di Angelantonio E, Bhupathiraju Sh N et al (2016) Body-mass index and all-cause mortality: individual-participant-data meta-analysis of 239 prospective studies in four continents. Lancet 388:776-786. https:// doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(16)30175-1, ord=62], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0063, section=References, kind=list, page=8, bbox={l=55.34076638503804, t=296.1599249535479, r=289.18163370716417, b=333.43728101532275}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Stefan N, Haring HU, Hu FB, Schulze MB (2013) Metabolically healthy obesity: epidemiology, mechanisms, and clinical implications. Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 1(2):152-162. https://doi.org/10. 1016/S2213-8587(13)70062-7, ord=63], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0064, section=References, kind=list, page=8, bbox={l=55.340767655267996, t=337.08673360753386, r=289.2239773309058, b=374.4233668133027}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Stefan N, Haring HU, Schulze MB (2018) Metabolically healthy obesity: the low-hanging fruit in obesity treatment? Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 6(3):249-258. https://doi.org/10.1016/S2213-8587(17) 30292-9, ord=64], PassageDto[id=E-0003-0065, section=References, kind=list, page=8, bbox={l=55.34923458475805, t=378.0728194055138, r=289.2154992233923, b=415.4094526112827}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=790.8660278320312, text=Neeland IJ, Poirier P, Despres JP (2018) Cardiovascular and metabolic heterogeneity of obesity: clinical challenges and implications for management. Circulation 137(13):1391-1406. https://doi.org/ 1