], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-002, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=Findings from several meta-analyses and recent large-scale cohort studies do not clearly support the notion that metabolically healthy obesity (MHO) subgroups, as currently defined, are protected from cardiometabolic complications., consumerDraft=Studies suggest that individuals with metabolically healthy obesity, as currently defined, may not be fully protected from heart and metabolic problems., quote=Interestingly, findings from several meta-analyses [8-11] and recent large-scale cohort studies [12-14] do not clearly support the notion that MHO subgroups, as currently defined, are protected from cardiometabolic complications (Table 1)., findingClass=PRIMARY, numbers=[], limitations=[lack of a uniform definition of the condition], population=Obese individuals categorized as metabolically healthy obesity (MHO)], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-003, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=According to prospective studies, the risk for cardiovascular disease (CVD) among metabolically unhealthy normal-weight (MUHNW) individuals is about 1.5-3-fold higher than among metabolically healthy individuals with normal weight, and this risk was higher than the relative risk in those with metabolically healthy obesity (MHO)., consumerDraft=People with normal weight but poor metabolic health have a 1.5-3 times higher risk of heart disease than metabolically healthy normal-weight individuals., quote=For example, according to prospective studies [8-10, 13, 14], risk for CVD among MUHNW individuals is about 1.5-3-fold higher than among metabolically healthy individuals with normal weight (Table 1); this risk was higher than the relative risk in those with MHO., findingClass=PRIMARY, numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=1.5-3, unit=fold, context=higher risk for CVD among MUHNW individuals]], limitations=[], population=Metabolically unhealthy normal-weight (MUHNW) individuals compared to metabolically healthy normal-weight individuals], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-004, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=In the European Prospective Investigation into Cancer and Nutrition (EPIC-CVD) study, only 20% of new cardiovascular disease (CVD) cases among normal-weight participants had the metabolic syndrome at baseline, which is a considerably smaller proportion than in overweight (52%) and obese (76%) individuals., consumerDraft=In one study, only 20% of normal-weight people who developed heart disease had metabolic syndrome, compared to 52% of overweight and 76% of obese individuals., quote=As an example, in the European Prospective Investigation into Cancer and Nutrition (EPIC-CVD) study, only 20% of incident CVD cases among normal-weight participants were observed in those with the metabolic syndrome at baseline; this is a considerably smaller proportion than in overweight (52%) and obese (76%) individuals [14]., findingClass=EXPLORATORY, numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=20%, unit=%, context=incident CVD cases among normal-weight participants observed in those with the metabolic syndrome at baseline], NumberRefDto[value=52%, unit=%, context=proportion of incident CVD cases in overweight individuals with metabolic syndrome at baseline], NumberRefDto[value=76%, unit=%, context=proportion of incident CVD cases in obese individuals with metabolic syndrome at baseline]], limitations=[The sensitivity of the metabolic syndrome to predict future CVD cases varies across the BMI range, and its absence is unlikely to rule out CVD risk in normal-weight individuals.], population=Normal-weight, overweight, and obese participants in the EPIC-CVD study], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-005, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=Cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk increases with increasing blood pressure, with risk gradients already observed within the blood pressure range considered to be normal., consumerDraft=Your risk of heart disease can increase even when your blood pressure is still considered within the normal range., quote=In addition, CVD risk increases with increasing blood pressure [17, 18], with risk gradients already observed within the blood pressure range considered to be normal [20]., findingClass=BACKGROUND, numbers=[], limitations=[], population=General population], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-006, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=Waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio are better predictors of cardiovascular events than BMI, although this predictive ability may differ across populations., consumerDraft=Measures like waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio may predict heart problems better than BMI, though this can vary by population., quote=Waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio better predict cardiovascular events than BMI, although this may differ across populations [1]., findingClass=BACKGROUND, numbers=[], limitations=[this may differ across populations], population=General population], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-007, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=Studies with follow-up of up to 10 years suggest that between one-third and one-half of individuals with metabolically healthy obesity (MHO) convert to an unhealthy phenotype over time., consumerDraft=Many studies show that between one-third and one-half of people with metabolically healthy obesity become metabolically unhealthy within 10 years., quote=This has been confirmed by studies with follow-up of up to 10 years, the majority of which suggest that between one-third and one-half of individuals with MHO convert to an unhealthy phenotype [13, 28-37] (Table 2)., findingClass=PRIMARY, numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=10, unit=years, context=follow-up duration], NumberRefDto[value=one-third, unit=, context=proportion of individuals with MHO converting to an unhealthy phenotype], NumberRefDto[value=one-half, unit=, context=proportion of individuals with MHO converting to an unhealthy phenotype]], limitations=[Very few studies have been conducted over longer time periods., The use of different definitions and measures of risk factors to define metabolic health complicates comparisons across studies.], population=Individuals with metabolically healthy obesity (MHO)], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-008, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=In a variety of cohort studies, approximately 60% of normal-weight individuals remained metabolically healthy after 10 years of follow-up, but in the Nurses' Health Study, only about 30% remained metabolically healthy after 20 years and about 15% after 30 years of follow-up., consumerDraft=About 60% of normal-weight individuals stayed metabolically healthy for 10 years, but this dropped to 15% after 30 years in one study., quote=While ~60% of individuals with normal weight were observed to remain metabolically healthy after 10 years of follow-up in a variety of cohort studies [13, 29, 32], only ~30% remained metabolically healthy after 20 years in the Nurses' Health Study and ~15% remained metabolically healthy after 30 years of follow-up [13]., findingClass=PRIMARY, numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=~60%, unit=%, context=proportion of normal-weight individuals remaining metabolically healthy after 10 years], NumberRefDto[value=10, unit=years, context=follow-up duration], NumberRefDto[value=~30%, unit=%, context=proportion of normal-weight individuals remaining metabolically healthy after 20 years in the Nurses' Health Study], NumberRefDto[value=20, unit=years, context=follow-up duration], NumberRefDto[value=~15%, unit=%, context=proportion of normal-weight individuals remaining metabolically healthy after 30 years in the Nurses' Health Study], NumberRefDto[value=30, unit=years, context=follow-up duration]], limitations=[The use of different definitions and measures of risk factors to define metabolic health complicates comparisons across studies.], population=Normal-weight individuals], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-009, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=In the Nurses' Health Study, individuals who maintained a metabolically healthy obesity (MHO) phenotype over 20 years still had a higher risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) over 10 years of follow-up when compared with individuals who were metabolically healthy and within the normal weight category over the same 20-year period., consumerDraft=Even obese individuals who stayed metabolically healthy for 20 years still had a higher risk of heart disease than metabolically healthy normal-weight individuals., quote=In the Nurses' Health Study, those who maintained an MHO phenotype over 20 years still had a higher risk of CVD over the 10 years' follow-up when compared with individuals who were metabolically healthy and within the normal weight category over the same time period [13]., findingClass=EXPLORATORY, numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=20, unit=years, context=duration MHO phenotype was maintained], NumberRefDto[value=10, unit=years, context=duration of CVD follow-up]], limitations=[The use of different definitions and measures of risk factors to define metabolic health complicates comparisons across studies.], population=Women who maintained MHO over 20 years compared to women who were metabolically healthy and normal weight over the same 20-year period], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-010, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=Conversion from a metabolically healthy to a metabolically unhealthy phenotype increased cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk similarly among obese individuals and individuals with normal weight., consumerDraft=If metabolic health worsens, the risk of heart disease increases similarly for both obese and normal-weight individuals., quote=On the other hand, conversion to a metabolically unhealthy phenotype increased CVD risk similarly among obese individuals and individuals with normal weight., findingClass=PRIMARY, numbers=[], limitations=[], population=Obese and normal-weight individuals who converted to an unhealthy phenotype], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-011, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=Conversion from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes has been related to higher baseline BMI or waist circumference and also to longer duration of obesity., consumerDraft=Higher baseline BMI, larger waist circumference, and longer duration of obesity are linked to becoming metabolically unhealthy., quote=Conversion from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes has been related to higher baseline BMI or waist circumference [28, 29, 36] and also to longer duration of obesity [38]., findingClass=PRIMARY, numbers=[], limitations=[], population=Individuals converting from metabolically healthy to unhealthy phenotypes], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-012, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=In a study of 85 Japanese-American men and women, the transition from metabolically healthy obesity (MHO) to metabolically unhealthy obesity (MUHO) was associated with greater visceral fat, but significant associations were not observed with greater abdominal subcutaneous fat., consumerDraft=In one study of 85 Japanese-American adults, becoming metabolically unhealthy was linked to more visceral fat, but not more abdominal subcutaneous fat., quote=Furthermore, in a study of 85 Japanese-American men and women, transition from MHO to metabolically unhealthy obesity (MUHO) was associated with greater visceral fat, but significant associations were not observed with greater abdominal subcutaneous fat [39]., findingClass=EXPLORATORY, numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=85, unit=, context=number of Japanese-American men and women in the study]], limitations=[small sample size, specific ethnicity], population=85 Japanese-American men and women], ApprovedClaimDto[claimId=C-0003-013, approvedCopy=null, scientificClaim=Landmark trials, such as The Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) trial, have shown that a diet rich in fruits and vegetables can reduce blood pressure., consumerDraft=Eating a diet rich in fruits and vegetables has been shown to help reduce blood pressure., quote=For example, l